ossibly less predictable.\n\nWait — is there any reason to prefer one over the other?\n\nNo immediate threats from White: White has isolated stones at D6, K12, N15 — all far away.\n\nSo no defensive concern.\n\nThus, either G8 or K8 is fine. Let\'s pick **K8** to build toward the right.\n\nBut wait — **is K8 already occupied**? Check: Occupied coordinates: D6, H8, I8, J8, K12, N15 → K8 is **not** occupied. ✅\n\nSimilarly, G8 is free.\n\nBut here\'s a better idea: instead of extending to 4-in-a-row immediately, could we play a move that creates a **fork** or **double threat**?\n\nFor example, playing **I9**? That would start a diagonal, but no support.\n\nOr **H9**? Vertical — only H8 above or below, nothing.\n\nNo.\n\nAlternatively, is there a way to create an **open 3 with two extensions**?\n\nCurrently, H8-J8 is a 3, with both G8 and K8 free → it\'s already an **open 3**.\n\nPlaying either end converts it to a **half-open 4**, which is still highly threatening.\n\nIn Gomoku, a 4-in-a-row must be blocked immediately.\n\nSo creating a 4-in-a-row is the strongest non-winning move.\n\nThus, **K8** or **G8** are both excellent.\n\nLet’s pick **K8** to continue the sequence to the right.\n\nReason:  \nExtending the existing 3-in-a-row (H8, I8, J8) to K8 creates a 4-in-a-row (H8–K8), which is a critical threat. White will be forced to respond by blocking at G8 on their next turn, giving Black the initiative. This move maximizes pressure and sets up for a potential win on the following move if White fails to block precisely. No immediate win exists, but this is the strongest forcing move available.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 8}'}
